{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Mayo (104 of 130 GTR NovaSeq 6000 records) keeps re-filing its 6000-labelled tests in 2025-26, so the biggest lab in the registry shows no sign of leaving the 6000.","detail":"Mayo records I opened were re-versioned on a roughly semi-annual cadence with the 6000 retained. 603740 (GATA2/arrhythmia-type panel): v.2 Apr 2023, v.3 Apr 2024, v.4 Apr 2025, v.5 Sep 2025, v.6 Apr 2026, all 'Illumina NovaSeq 6000'. 614961 (Expanded Pancreatitis panel) was registered Jul 2025, with updates Sep 2025 and Mar 2026, all on the 6000. A new Mayo record registered in Jul 2025 still names the 6000, which says new tests are being filed on it. Mayo has 0 X records in the 12-hit X set. This is one lab's records, not volume.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gtr/tests/603740/","date":"2026-04-10","source_name":"NCBI GTR","source_type":"dataset","quote":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","strength":"medium","why_new":"Adds dated version histories showing Mayo's re-filing cadence on the 6000, beyond the known 104/130 count."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Outside Mayo, large commercial and academic oncology labs still declare the 6000 on records registered or reviewed in 2024-26, and none of them has a X record.","detail":"Tempus xT (CHI, CLIA 14D2114007; 558436), Tempus xR (RTP and CHI), and Tempus xF (RTP and CHI) all appear in the 6000 set. Tempus xT RTP 613444 reads 'Novaseq 6000' for both MSI and sequence analysis; it was registered 6 Nov 2024 with a lab annual review on 12 Nov 2025. Tempus xF CHI 569040 reads 'Illumina NovaSeq 6000 System' and was last updated 7 Nov 2024 (v.6, after updates in 2020-22). I found no Tempus record in the X set. These are lab declarations, not Gb shares.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gtr/tests/613444/","date":"2025-11-12","source_name":"NCBI GTR","source_type":"dataset","quote":"Novaseq 6000","strength":"medium","why_new":"Known list says only that Tempus job postings and a GTR xT record exist. This adds the full xT/xR/xF set with dates and shows no X counterpart."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Labcorp's new Plasma Detect MRD tests, registered Dec 2025, list the NovaSeq 6000Dx as the platform, so a new MRD product is being filed on the 6000 line.","detail":"Plasma Detect Genome MRD-Baseline (619625) and Plasma Detect MRD-Monitoring (619713), Molecular Oncology Laboratory, LabCorp (CLIA 34D1008914), were registered 8 Dec 2025. The Test Platform field reads 'illumina NovaSeq 6000 Dx'. The Instrument column shows only the sonicator. This is a regulated-grade 6000Dx platform on a brand-new whole-genome MRD product. It supports the idea that new volume at a large lab starts on the 6000Dx, not the X. Whether that reflects a validation lag or a real plan to stay is unknown.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gtr/tests/619625/","date":"2025-12-08","source_name":"NCBI GTR","source_type":"dataset","quote":"illumina NovaSeq 6000 Dx","strength":"medium","why_new":"Not in known list: Labcorp WGS-MRD filed on the 6000Dx in Dec 2025."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"UCSF500 and JAX Genome, two academic clinical tests, remain on the 6000 in current records.","detail":"UCSF500 Cancer Gene Panel (591895) reads 'Illumina NovaSeq 6000 system' and was last updated 31 Jan 2026 (v.2, first registered 6 Oct 2020), so it was re-filed on the 6000 in 2026. JAX Genome (613393, CLIA 07D2061461) reads 'Illumina NovaSeq 6000', was registered 7 Oct 2024, with a lab annual review on 28 Aug 2026. A genome-scale clinical test registered in late 2024 on the 6000 is notable.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gtr/tests/591895/","date":"2026-01-31","source_name":"NCBI GTR","source_type":"dataset","quote":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 system","strength":"medium","why_new":"Names specific academic labs and dated 2026 re-filing absent from known evidence."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"against","claim":"A leading NIPT lab (Labcorp's Integrated Genetics-Sequenom, MaterniT) lists the NovaSeq X Plus on all four of its records, so the NIPT cohort moved to the X.","detail":"MaterniT21 (501985), MaterniT GENOME (569955), MaterniT21 Genome Add On (593063) and its Redraw (593064), CLIA 05D2015356, all read 'Novaseq X Plus'. They were last updated 1 Nov 2024. 501985 had v.1-2 in Nov 2013, v.3 in Jan 2020 and v.4 in Nov 2024. No MaterniT record is in the 6000 set. This supports the known Thaysen cohort 4 claim that NIPT mostly moved, and it means the 6000 tail is not in NIPT. It also means the NIPT price cut has already been absorbed.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gtr/tests/501985/","date":"2024-11-01","source_name":"NCBI GTR","source_type":"dataset","quote":"Novaseq X Plus","strength":"medium","why_new":"Known note says only that 'Labcorp-owned Integrated Genetics (MaterniT)' appears among the X hits. This gives all four record IDs, dates and the version history."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"against","claim":"Michigan's Molecular Genetics Laboratory re-versioned six hereditary-gene records to the NovaSeq X Plus on 24-25 Jun 2026, so a US academic clinical lab did switch in 2026.","detail":"Michigan (CLIA 23D1088637) has 6 of the 12 X hits: BRCA1/2, ATP7B, CHD7, TP53, CFTR sequencing and GDI1, plus CF carrier screening 507010 ('Illumina NovaSeqX'; v.4 on 25 Jun 2026 after v.3 in Jul 2024). ATP7B 508735 is v.5, last updated 24 Jun 2026, and BRCA1/2 509980 is v.5, also 24 Jun 2026. The prior version of 508735 (v.4, 27 Jun 2025) read 'Illumina NextSeq 550', so this switch was from the mid-throughput NextSeq, not the 6000. A 'NovaSeq 6000 AND Michigan' search returns 0.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gtr/tests/508735/","date":"2026-06-24","source_name":"NCBI GTR","source_type":"dataset","quote":"Illumina NovaSeq X Plus","strength":"medium","why_new":"Known list has only the aggregate '12 X hits'. This identifies half as one lab, the dates, and shows the previous platform was a NextSeq 550."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"against","claim":"Most of the 12 X hits are not independent evidence of clinical X conversion, because they come from a few labs and one is a false positive.","detail":"The 12 'NovaSeq X' hits are 6 Michigan records (50%), 4 Integrated Genetics MaterniT records (33%), plus one Indian male-infertility panel (619856) and one Mass General Brigham polygenic risk test (619622), whose Instrument field says 'Not provided' (the text mentions Broad Clinical Labs sequencing). So only 10 of 12 names the X in a verified field, from 2 US labs. Broadening to 'NovaSeq X Plus OR NovaSeqX OR NovaSeq X' gives 14 hits: it adds 507010 (Michigan) and 618996 (UW SUDY exome, Instrument 'Not provided', registered 29 Sep 2025). GTR is self-reported and lightly maintained, so the 130 vs 12 ratio overstates the 6000's share.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gtr&term=%22NovaSeq%20X%20Plus%22%20OR%20NovaSeqX%20OR%20%22NovaSeq%20X%22","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GTR E-utilities","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Corrects the 11:1 ratio: de-duplicates the X hits and shows they cluster in two labs."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"The 6000 set outside Mayo is only about 26 records and spans oncology (Tempus, UCSF, JAX, Labcorp), rare disease and small foreign labs.","detail":"130 6000 hits minus 104 Mayo = 26. I identified: Tempus xT/xR/xF (7 records, 3 sites incl. 558436 CHI and 613445 RTP), Labcorp Plasma Detect (2), UCSF500 (1), JAX Genome (1), a Bangladesh whole-exome test from NeuroGen (595969), 2 Burdwan University (India) rare-disease tests (613590, 613591), UW SUDY exome (618996, dual hit), and Providence 'ProvSeq 523' (556857). About 6 IDs (e.g. 576389 'NeXT Dx', 619621, 613192, 613130, 611987) could not be resolved because the summaries were truncated. By test category the non-Mayo tail is mostly tissue/liquid oncology and exome, not NIPT.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gtr&term=%22NovaSeq%206000%22&retmax=200","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GTR E-utilities","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Gives the category breakdown that the known list calls out as the next step (Mayo split out)."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"NeXT Dx (Natera-competitor Personalis/Tempus-bound WGS-style test) and ProvSeq 523 are listed on the 6000, so WGS-adjacent and 500-gene oncology products are being built on it.","detail":"576389 'NeXT Dx' and 556857 'ProvSeq 523' (Providence) are in the 6000 set. I could not read their Instrument fields or dates, because the batch summary truncated. Providence is the same group that validated 30x clinical genomes on the 6000 in 2025 (known). Needs a manual open of both records to confirm.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gtr/tests/576389/","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GTR","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Adds two named products to the 6000 tail; both unverified."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"against","claim":"Labcorp registering new MRD tests in Dec 2025 shows the large labs are adding WGS-MRD products, which cuts against 'WGS and MRD will not become a meaningful mix'.","detail":"Plasma Detect Genome MRD-Baseline and Plasma Detect MRD-Monitoring were registered 8 Dec 2025. A 'Genome MRD' baseline step implies whole-genome depth per patient. Tempus is similarly acquiring Personalis (known). The count is two new product records, so it is a watch-out, not a volume measure.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gtr/tests/619713/","date":"2025-12-08","source_name":"NCBI GTR","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"New: Labcorp genome-MRD product launch evidenced in registry."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Mayo's 6000 records are mostly hereditary gene panels and single-gene full-gene tests, so the registry's 6000 tail is low-volume rare-disease testing.","detail":"Opened IDs included CFTR, MECP2, GATA2, BTK, NOTCH3, VHL, STK11, BAP1, FH, NF1, and neuromuscular, arrhythmia, SCID, nephrology and epilepsy panels (Mayo CLIA 24D0404292/24D1040592). Counted from batches: 16 in IDs 603733-603748, 14 in 597342-597355, plus 593005/593088/593144 and others. This matches the F830 view that low-volume owners have little reason to convert. These are per-test records and carry no volume.","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gtr/tests/603586/","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GTR","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Categorizes Mayo's 80% share by test type."}
